WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.609 --> 00:00:03.803
- [Narrator] So, these are jellied eels.

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:05.607
Eels in jelly.

00:00:05.607 --> 00:00:08.753
- [Booming Voice] Jellied eels!

00:00:08.753 --> 00:00:10.916
- [Narrator] And these are ells with no jelly,

00:00:10.916 --> 00:00:11.969
like, loads of eels,

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:14.131
drawers of eels!

00:00:14.131 --> 00:00:15.398
Hold on to your seats!

00:00:15.398 --> 00:00:16.846
This might get a bit weird.

00:00:16.846 --> 00:00:19.429
(quirky music)

00:00:20.672 --> 00:00:24.242
So, we’ve got this giant
giggly bowl of jellied eels

00:00:24.242 --> 00:00:26.156
(animated piano music)
from this guy.

00:00:26.156 --> 00:00:27.273
- My name is John Chilvers.

00:00:27.273 --> 00:00:28.688
I work for Micks Eel Supply

00:00:28.688 --> 00:00:30.546
in Billingsgate Fish Market.

00:00:30.546 --> 00:00:34.040
We supply live eels to the
catering trade, public.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.235
I've been selling eels
for 33 years

00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:37.725
since I left school.

00:00:37.725 --> 00:00:39.649
I have some lovely eels here.

00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:42.012
These are all farmed from Holland.

00:00:42.012 --> 00:00:46.194
They're like the Rolls
Royce of the jellied eels.

00:00:46.194 --> 00:00:48.257
They're very, very slippery

00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:50.193
like some of my customers.
(animated piano music)

00:00:50.193 --> 00:00:51.760
- [Narrator] Back in the 18th century,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.893
London's River Thames was
crawling with these eels.

00:00:54.893 --> 00:00:58.112
Nowadays, John is one of
the last few eel suppliers

00:00:58.112 --> 00:00:59.359
in London.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:02.180
So John, why were they so popular?

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:03.013
- You need to speak to

00:01:03.013 --> 00:01:03.846
one of the older fish mongers

00:01:03.846 --> 00:01:05.855
for that.
(animated piano music)

00:01:05.855 --> 00:01:06.980
- [Narrator] Oh!

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.521
Uh, okay, then.

00:01:08.521 --> 00:01:10.878
- [Man] I'm 72 years old,
and I eat eels every day.

00:01:10.878 --> 00:01:11.930
- [Narrator] Perfect!

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.136
So, Frank of Bradley's Fish Supply,

00:01:14.136 --> 00:01:16.536
what made them so popular back then?

00:01:16.536 --> 00:01:18.727
- In those days, it was no brainer:

00:01:18.727 --> 00:01:19.989
Everybody ate jellied eels.

00:01:19.989 --> 00:01:21.759
It was an easy source to get.

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:22.592
Very simple.

00:01:22.592 --> 00:01:25.334
You’d put a hook out and
they catch their self.

00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:26.571
Eels are really edible.

00:01:26.571 --> 00:01:28.254
And they were full of goodness.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:29.266
The people didn't realize it then

00:01:29.266 --> 00:01:31.660
but they were, they were
eating a real good product.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.498
And we didn't have Indian, Chinese take-away,

00:01:34.498 --> 00:01:35.790
kabobs, everything else.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.445
It was a source of food.

00:01:38.445 --> 00:01:40.077
- [Narrator] So, how do you make them?

00:01:40.077 --> 00:01:40.910
- Quite easy to prepare.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:42.451
Head off, guts out,

00:01:42.451 --> 00:01:43.676
chopped in pieces,

00:01:43.676 --> 00:01:44.722
then we cooked them.

00:01:44.722 --> 00:01:47.615
The process of cooking is more of an art.

00:01:47.615 --> 00:01:49.425
You need them to be very soft.

00:01:49.425 --> 00:01:50.891
Now if you go past being soft,

00:01:50.891 --> 00:01:51.985
all you've got is mash.

00:01:51.985 --> 00:01:53.014
- [Narrator] Thanks, Frank.

00:01:53.014 --> 00:01:53.847
(bell rings)

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:55.107
So, pie shop owner, Kane:

00:01:55.107 --> 00:01:56.881
What do they go well with?

00:01:56.881 --> 00:01:59.944
- Jellied eels and stewed
eels are very traditional

00:01:59.944 --> 00:02:03.861
with pie, mash, and liquor
in the East End of London.

00:02:03.861 --> 00:02:05.270
So, you know, it’s not for everybody.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.796
The world's changing a little bit.

00:02:06.796 --> 00:02:09.541
You do either like 'em or hate them.

00:02:09.541 --> 00:02:10.709
Probably in the old days,

00:02:10.709 --> 00:02:14.876
we would go through maybe 50
or 60 bowls of eels a week.

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:18.305
Probably now, we might do 10 or 12.

00:02:18.305 --> 00:02:21.202
So, if you think about food around the world,

00:02:21.202 --> 00:02:23.648
you think about Paris and their baguettes.

00:02:23.648 --> 00:02:25.364
New York, you think about the pizzas,

00:02:25.364 --> 00:02:26.658
the New York cheesecakes.

00:02:26.658 --> 00:02:28.754
That's what they're synonymous for.

00:02:28.754 --> 00:02:31.068
London, the traditional food in London is

00:02:31.068 --> 00:02:32.320
pie, mash and eels.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.943
That is our staple diet.

00:02:34.943 --> 00:02:36.031
- [Narrator] And the taste?

00:02:36.031 --> 00:02:36.921
- [Kane] It's like eating,

00:02:36.921 --> 00:02:38.473
basically, a chunky white fish.

00:02:38.473 --> 00:02:41.068
(perky music)
(jelly jiggling)

00:02:41.068 --> 00:02:43.313
(perky music)

00:02:43.313 --> 00:02:47.333
♫ It must be love
- [Man] Mmm!

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:51.649
♫ It must be love
- [Man] Mmm!

00:02:51.649 --> 00:02:55.816
♫ It must be love ♫
- [Man] Mmm!

